# The candle and the jar: a flame does not use up ALL the oxygen
#
# Everyone is taught that a candle under a jar "uses up the oxygen" and
# goes out. Watch the numbers here: it goes out with roughly three quarters
# of the oxygen still in the jar. A flame does not need SOME oxygen — it
# needs air that is RICH in it, about one part in six, and below that it
# quits with plenty left over.
register lv2

# 1. A candle in the open air. Nothing limits it, so it burns away
#    completely — 5 g of wax is about 210 kJ, which is a lot of energy
#    for something you can hold in one hand.
add v1 candle_wax 5g
ignite v1
inspect v1

# 2. The same candle under a jar. `regulate` is the honest boundary for a
#    jar sitting on a bench: the gas cannot leave, but it can push, so
#    nothing bursts. Watch the oxygen line before and after.
new beaker
add v2 candle_wax 5g
regulate v2 1atm 1L
inspect v2
ignite v2
inspect v2

# Read what just happened. Barely any wax burned, the flame went out, and
# there is still oxygen in the jar — it fell from 21% of the gas to 16%,
# and that was enough to stop it. Carbon dioxide and water took the place
# of the oxygen that went.

# 3. A fire extinguisher, which is a bottle of carbon dioxide. Nothing is
#    taken away from the candle: the wax is all still there, and so is the
#    oxygen. The carbon dioxide only takes up room, and now the flame
#    cannot start at all.
new beaker
add v3 candle_wax 5g
regulate v3 1atm 1L
add v3 CO2 0.02mol
ignite v3
inspect v3

# 4. Paper burns too, and it is the same chemistry with a different fuel:
#    cellulose instead of wax. Paper catches at 506 K — which is 451
#    degrees Fahrenheit, and the novel's title is a real measurement.
#    Note how much less heat the same mass gives than wax does.
new beaker
add v4 paper 5g
ignite v4
inspect v4

# What this does not model: the wick, the melt pool, the light. A real
# candle burns wax VAPOUR drawn up a wick, slowly; this bench burns what
# the air allows all at once and does not model the clock. There is no
# soot, no smoke, no char and no carbon monoxide — burning here is always
# complete, straight to carbon dioxide and water. And the 16% limit is one
# teaching number standing in for something that really depends on the
# fuel, the wick and the shape of the jar.
